From e3978b9821a2f60619d71bb75d2ec647a87eff8e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 23:49:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tools/dvbridge, settings: a picture scaled to the screen
 steps down to the converted transport

A picture scaled up to the screen (a 1080p video) goes out in the source
transport since this version, scaled in the composer's representation: more
faithful than converted to BT.2020 (as earlier builds sent it) and more GPU
time (on Mesa's zink, 35 ms against 19 to 20 for the direct way of a 1080p
picture). Its ladder of the fidelity step-down had nothing left: the source
transport scales up no composed plane, and levels 1 to 3 changed nothing for
a picture without a residual (0x1). A graphics chip that kept up with such a
video converted could then miss its refreshes with no way down.

Now, from level 1 on, such a picture goes out converted to BT.2020, as
earlier builds sent it, at that level: level 1 is then the converted
picture of before (for a picture without a residual, exactly that of level
0 converted), level 3 scales its composed planes up bilinearly. Its ladder is
0xb (0xf with a residual) at every level it is drawn at, so that the
step-down keeps one ladder for it; source_why says why it went out
converted, which Kodi logs once. Level 0 does not change. The help of the
setting says it, in English and Spanish.

Tested with fidbench at half size (1080p pictures of a profile 7 FEL stream
scaled to the raster), with and without the enhancement layer: level 0 the
same bytes as before (24 outputs each); at levels 1, 2 and 3, the same bytes
as the converted transport at that level; without the layer, level 1 the
same as the converted transport at level 0. The skin check passes.
---
 .../resources/strings.po                      |  2 +-
 .../resource.language.en_gb/strings.po        |  2 +-
 .../resource.language.es_ar/strings.po        |  4 +--
 .../resource.language.es_es/strings.po        |  4 +--
 .../resource.language.es_mx/strings.po        |  4 +--
 tools/dvbridge/dvbridge_render.c              | 26 +++++++++++++++++--
 tools/dvbridge/dvbridge_render.h              |  5 +++-
 7 files changed, 36 insertions(+), 11 deletions(-)

diff --git a/addons/resource.language.en_gb/resources/strings.po b/addons/resource.language.en_gb/resources/strings.po
index ab1b7e1..5ab3df6 100644
--- a/addons/resource.language.en_gb/resources/strings.po
+++ b/addons/resource.language.en_gb/resources/strings.po
@@ -25750,7 +25750,7 @@ msgstr ""
 #. Hades: help of setting "videoscreen.hadesdvstepmode"; translated in Estuary (#31748)
 #: system/settings/settings.xml
 msgctxt "#39963"
-msgid "Where the graphics card cannot prepare a native Dolby Vision picture in the time of a frame, even drawn the faster way and a frame ahead (an integrated one with 4K Blu-ray video and its enhancement layer), the picture can be rebuilt a little less precisely: some of its planes are scaled up with a simpler filter. In test pictures most of the pixels that change do so by the smallest step of a 12-bit picture, none by more than 16 before the last step, which can move a few pixels at edges of strong colour further. Automatic: one step at a time and only as far as the video needs to play smoothly; graphics that keep up always show the exact picture, and what is found is kept for this graphics card and build. Never: always the exact picture, even if the video stutters. Level 1, 2, 3: that level for every frame, to measure what it saves. A level changes only the frames that the renderer says it changes (with the composer of the specification, levels 1 and 2 only those that rebuild the residual of a full enhancement layer, FEL; with libplacebo's reshaping, levels 2 and 3), and a frame that it does not change is drawn at the highest level under it that changes it, or exactly, as the player information then says. While a level is set nothing that is measured is kept. A level set in advancedsettings.xml (dvstepdown) comes first. Applies from the next video."
+msgid "Where the graphics card cannot prepare a native Dolby Vision picture in the time of a frame, even drawn the faster way and a frame ahead (an integrated one with 4K Blu-ray video and its enhancement layer), the picture can be rebuilt a little less precisely: some of its planes are scaled up with a simpler filter. In test pictures most of the pixels that change do so by the smallest step of a 12-bit picture, none by more than 16 before the last step, which can move a few pixels at edges of strong colour further. Automatic: one step at a time and only as far as the video needs to play smoothly; graphics that keep up always show the exact picture, and what is found is kept for this graphics card and build. Never: always the exact picture, even if the video stutters. Level 1, 2, 3: that level for every frame, to measure what it saves. A level changes only the frames that the renderer says it changes (with the composer of the specification, levels 1 and 2 only those that rebuild the residual of a full enhancement layer, FEL; with libplacebo's reshaping, levels 2 and 3; a picture scaled to the screen, such as a 1080p video, goes out converted to BT.2020 from level 1 on, as earlier builds sent it), and a frame that it does not change is drawn at the highest level under it that changes it, or exactly, as the player information then says. While a level is set nothing that is measured is kept. A level set in advancedsettings.xml (dvstepdown) comes first. Applies from the next video."
 msgstr ""
 
